'''''Crataegus pinnatifida''''', also known as '''mountain hawthorn''',<ref>{{Cite book|url=http://www.forest.go.kr/kna/special/download/English_Names_for_Korean_Native_Plants.pdf|title=English Names for Korean Native Plants|publisher=[[Korea National Arboretum]]|year=2015|isbn=978-89-97450-98-5|location=Pocheon|page=424|access-date=24 December 2016|via=[[Korea Forest Service]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170525105020/http://www.forest.go.kr/kna/special/download/English_Names_for_Korean_Native_Plants.pdf|archive-date=25 May 2017}}</ref> '''Chinese haw''',<ref name=PFAF>{{citation |url=http://www.pfaf.org/user/Plant.aspx?LatinName=Crataegus+pinnatifida |title=Plants for a Future |access-date=20 October 2015}}</ref> '''Chinese hawthorn''' or '''Chinese hawberry''',<ref>{{cite book|title=Genetics and genomics of Rosaceae|year=2008|publisher=Springer|location=New York|isbn=978-0-387-77490-9|page=11|author1=Hummer, K.E. |author2=Janick, J. |editor1=Folta, Kevin M. |editor-link1=Kevin Folta|editor2=Gardiner, Susan E. }}</ref><ref>{{cite book|last=Flint|first=Harrison L.|title=Landscape plants for eastern North America: exclusive of Florida and the immediate Gulf Coast |year=1997 |publisher=Wiley |location=New York |isbn=978-0-471-59919-7|page=158}}</ref> refers to a small to medium-sized tree, as well as the fruit of the tree. The fruit is bright red, {{convert|1.5|in|abbr=on}} in diameter.

==Use==

===Culinary use===
In northern Chinese cuisine, ripe ''C. pinnatifida'' fruits are used in the desserts ''[[tanghulu]]'' and ''[[shanzhagao]]''. It is also used to make the traditional candies [[haw flakes]] and [[haw rolls]], as well as candied fruit slices, jam, jelly, and wine. It is also traditionally used as a finishing ingredient in Cantonese [[sweet and sour#Hong Kong/Cantonese|sweet and sour sauce]], although it has since been partially supplanted by [[ketchup]].<ref>{{cite web |first1=Stephanie|last1=Li|first2=Chris|last2=Thomas|publisher= Chinese Cooking Demystified |date= 26 November 2018 |title= Old School Sweet and Sour Pork, without Ketchup (山楂咕噜肉)|language= en |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_9TaR8racD8  |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211221/_9TaR8racD8 |archive-date=2021-12-21 |url-status=live|access-date= 2 February 2019  |via=[[YouTube]] }}{{cbignore}}
</ref>

===Traditional medicine===
In [[traditional Chinese medicine]], the dried fruits of ''C. pinnatifida'' have been used as a digestive aid.<ref name=itmonline>{{cite web |author=Dharmananda S. |title=Hawthorn (''Crataegus''). Food and Medicine in China |publisher=Institute of Traditional Medicine Online |volume=January |year=2004 |url=http://www.itmonline.org/arts/crataegus.htm }}</ref>

